{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The behavior looks sound and well tested. The gap is that one piece of the implementation has no `{impl}` marker for this requirement.\n\n**What is implemented correctly**\n- `UsageLog::prepared` (`src/authoring/usage.rs`) records one `InputUsage` per prepared input.\n  - Each record carries an origin (`mandatory`, or the tool name such as `habeas` or `ripgrep`) and a kind (`outline`, `requirements`, `definition`, or `builtin`).\n  - It stores UTF-8 bytes (summed `str::len` plus newlines) and lines (the length of the text array).\n  - It stores `components`, which is the number of matched declarations for a requirements input and 1 otherwise.\n  - It stores `unique`, which is false when an identical input was already seen, so repeated reads are distinguished.\n- Prepared versus sent is tracked with `submitted_in_request` (set in `begin_request`) and `sent_in_request` (set in `end_request` only when the response carried usage). A failed or retried request therefore does not mark inputs as sent.\n- `UsageLog::tool`, `skipped_tool` and the `tool_failures` map count successful and failed tool calls, including `list_files`.\n- `list_files`, `outline` and `references` return `inputs: Vec::new()` in `src/authoring/tools.rs`. Directory listings therefore count as tool calls but never become inputs in the usage log or the evidence.\n- Additional inputs from `habeas` and `ripgrep` are recorded through `converse`, which calls `usage.prepared(call.function.name, &delivery.inputs)`.\n- The follow-up conversations record their own mandatory inputs via `followup_sources`, which is marked.\n\n**Tests**\n- The unit test `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` covers kind, bytes (including a multibyte character), lines, components, unique versus repeated, origin, and the null → submitted → sent progression.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` covers a failed request, so the input is submitted but not sent, followed by a successful retry.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` covers `list_files` counted as a tool call with no inputs, and failed versus skipped calls.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` covers retries.\n- The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` covers an end-to-end repeated `habeas` read (`unique == false`, `sent_in_request == 1`), a failed read, `list_files` counted in `tool_call_count`, no `.` or `absent.txt` in the evidence, and mandatory definition inputs with bytes > 0.\n- Minor coverage thinness: the CLI test does not assert directly that the usage inputs omit the `list_files` listing, and it does not check `ripgrep`-origin usage inputs. The unit tests and the code structure cover both points.\n\n**Gap**\n- `assess_subject` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`, lines 462–538) contains the call that records the first conversation's mandatory inputs: `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", sources.mandatory_inputs())`.\n- Its marker block lists `confirmation`, `usage_storage` and `mandatory_inputs`, but not `usage_inputs`.\n- `configured_record` does carry `{impl}` for `usage_inputs`, but it only prepares sources and calls `assess_subject`. It does not record usage.\n- The code that implements part of this requirement is therefore not marked as implementing it.\n- Under the assessment rule, unmarked implementing code is a gap. Nothing in the sources acknowledges it with a FIXME.\n- `tool_name`, which feeds the tool-call counts that the file-discovery obligation depends on, is marked only for `usage_requests` and `usage_storage`. This is a smaller omission.",
  "references": "3fc6c86a78c0f7d2f7405cefcb10a220d2f2fbdbac9dcbcb8125505ff922dbd9",
